spending hours in queues has become the  norm across sri lanka  starting with food rice sugar and milk  powder now it's cooking gas and diesel  that are in short supply  i haven't eaten from the morning and i  can't even go drink some water i would  lose my place otherwise  after spending so many hours in queues  we can only run about two and a half  days we can't keep doing this so we  appeal for this problem to be solved  the shortages are a result of the  government not having enough dollars to  pay for imports this is because its  foreign earnings and reserves have been  rapidly declining since 2019.  huge debt repayments have eaten into a  big chunk of available resources  the crisis has affected all sectors  including public transport  operators say they are struggling to  survive from day to day  on the one hand there's no electricity  they cut power for seven and a half  hours the diesel stocks that come half  is given for power generation it's only  half the stock that's used for  everything else after months of coping  people are protesting here in chile  north of colombo fishermen complain they  don't have fuel for their boats  my husband can't go to sea there's no  diesel i hope those responsible are  struck by lightning  in colombo a silent protest  enough is enough says one banner  stop ruining my country reads another  months of skyrocketing prices and  shortages of food medicine cooking gas  and fuel have made sri lankans  frustrated and angry  to make matters worse  they've suffered through the worst power  cuts to be imposed in more than 25 years  seven and a half hour interruptions a  day are causing chaos among all segments  of the population  sri lanka's public utilities regulator  says it has been pushing to solve the  power crisis this has become a national  issue so  necessary  the attention has not been paid by the  political hierarchy then and now and the  other authorities so we have been  fighting you know  with almost everyone to sort out this  issue increasing prices and shortages  are not unique to sri lanka but people  here say their leaders have aggravated  the situation through years of  corruption and mismanagement  fernandez al jazeera colombo
 

